00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.600
Hello and welcome back. In this week, you learn about optimization algorithms that will enable you to train your neural networks much faster.

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:12.640
You've heard me say before that applied machine learning is a highly empirical process,

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:17.900
it's highly iterative process in which you just have to train a lot of models to find one that works really well.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:20.700
So it really helps to really train models quickly.

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:23.800
One thing that makes it more difficult is that deep learning

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:29.600
tends to work best in the regime of big data when you're able to train your neural networks on a huge data set.

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:32.720
And training on the large data set is just slow.

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:38.400
So what you find is that having fast optimization algorithms, having good optimization algorithms,

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:41.440
can really speed up the efficiency of you and your team.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:45.680
So let's get started by talking about mini-batch gradient descent.

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:50.720
You've learned previously that vectorization allows you to efficiently compute on all m

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:56.320
examples that allows you to process your whole training set without an explicit for loop.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:01.120
So that's why we would take our training examples and stack them into this

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:10.880
huge matrix, capital X, so X1, X2, X3, you know, and then eventually it goes up to Xm.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:22.160
If you have m training examples and similarly for y, this is y1, y2, y3, and so on up to ym.

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:28.960
So the dimension of X was nx by m and this was 1 by m.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:34.560
Vectorization allows you to process all m examples quickly, relatively quickly.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:37.840
If m is very large, then it can still be slow.

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:43.360
So for example, what if m was 5 million or 50 million or even bigger.

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:48.640
With the implementation of gradient descent on your whole training set,

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:51.680
what you have to do is you have to process your entire training set

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:54.880
before you take one little step of gradient descent.

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:58.400
And then you have to process your entire training set of 5 million training examples again

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:00.720
before you take another little step of gradient descent.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:05.760
So it turns out that you can get a faster algorithm if you let gradient descent

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:09.200
start to make some progress even before you finish processing

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:13.120
your entire, your giant training sets of 5 million examples.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:16.720
In particular, here's what you can do.

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:19.760
Let's say that you split up your training set into smaller,

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:22.080
your little baby training sets.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:25.360
And these baby training sets are called mini batches.

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:34.340
And let's say each of your baby training sets have just 1,000 examples each.

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:42.340
So you take X1 through X1000 and you call that your first little baby training set,

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:43.860
also called a mini batch.

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:52.500
And then you take the next 1000 examples, X10001 through X2000.

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:56.020
That's the next 1000 examples and call the next one and so on.

00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:58.580
And I'm going to introduce a new notation.

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:04.500
I'm going to call this X super strip with curly braces 1.

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:12.020
And I'm going to call this X super stripped with curly braces 2.

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:15.140
Now, if you have 5 million training examples total,

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:18.340
and each of these little mini batches has a thousand examples,

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:21.140
that means you have 5,000 of these because, you know,

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:24.420
5,000 times 1,000 equals 5 million.

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:30.100
So altogether you would have 5,000 of these mini batches.

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:34.020
So it ends with X super stripped curly braces 5,000.

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:37.140
And then similarly, you do the same thing for Y.

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:42.100
You'd also split up your training data for Y accordingly.

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:44.020
So you call that Y1.

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:50.020
Then this is Y10001 through Y2000.

00:03:50.900 --> 00:04:00.100
This becomes called Y2 and so on until you have Y5000.

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:12.540
So now, mini batch number T is going to be comprised of XT and YT.

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:15.500
And that is a thousand training examples.

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:17.580
With the corresponding input output pairs.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:22.220
Before moving on, just to make sure our notation is clear,

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:25.340
we have previously used super stripped round brackets I

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:26.780
to index in the training set.

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:29.340
So XI is the I of training example.

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:35.020
We use super stripped square brackets L to index into the different layers of a neural network.

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:41.340
So ZL comes from the Z values for the Lth layer of the neural network.

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:47.820
And here we're introducing the curly brackets T to index into different mini batches.

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:51.180
So you have XT, YT.

00:04:52.140 --> 00:05:00.860
And to check your understanding of these, what's the dimension, right, of XT and YT?

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:04.620
Well, X is NX by M.

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:10.140
So if X1 is a thousand training examples or the X values for a thousand examples,

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:13.420
then this dimension should be NX by 1000.

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:19.180
And X2 should also be NX by 1000 and so on.

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:22.700
So all of these should have dimension NX by 1000.

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:27.020
And these should have dimension 1 by 1000.

00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:34.190
To explain the name of this algorithm,

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:38.670
batch gradient descent refers to the gradient descent algorithm

00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:40.190
we've been talking about previously,

00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:43.070
where you process your entire training set all at the same time.

00:05:43.630 --> 00:05:49.390
And the name comes from viewing that as processing your entire batch of training examples

00:05:49.390 --> 00:05:50.350
all at the same time.

00:05:50.350 --> 00:05:53.150
I'm not just a great name, but that's just what it's called.

00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:57.470
Mini batch gradient descent, in contrast, refers to the algorithm,

00:05:57.470 --> 00:05:59.070
which we'll talk about on the next slide,

00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:04.590
in which you process a single mini batch, XT, YT, at the same time,

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:08.110
rather than processing your entire training set X, Y at the same time.

00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:12.190
So let's see how mini batch gradient descent works.

00:06:12.190 --> 00:06:15.150
To run mini batch gradient descent on your training set,

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:24.750
you would run for T equals 1 to 5000, because we had 5000 mini batches of size 1000 each.

00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:27.790
And what you're going to do inside the for loop is basically

00:06:27.790 --> 00:06:39.440
implement one step of gradient descent using XT, YT.

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:45.680
And it's as if you had a training set of size 1000 examples.

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:52.000
And it was as if you were to implement the algorithm you already familiar with,

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:56.960
but just on this, you know, little training set size of M equals 1000.

00:06:56.960 --> 00:07:00.880
Rather than having an explicit for loop over all 1000 examples,

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:05.760
you would use vectorization to process all 1000 examples, sort of all at the same time.

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:08.560
So let's write this out.

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:15.170
First, you implement for a prop on the inputs.

00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:16.850
So just on XT.

00:07:17.570 --> 00:07:23.570
And you do that by implementing, you know, Z1 equals W1.

00:07:24.290 --> 00:07:27.650
Now, previously, we just have X there, right?

00:07:27.650 --> 00:07:29.970
But now you aren't processing entire training set,

00:07:29.970 --> 00:07:31.730
you're just processing the first mini batch.

00:07:31.730 --> 00:07:36.770
So this becomes XT when you're processing mini batch T.

00:07:37.010 --> 00:07:44.130
And then you would have A1 equals G1 of Z1.

00:07:44.850 --> 00:07:49.170
Right, this is your capital Z, since we're this is actually a vectorized implementation.

00:07:50.530 --> 00:08:01.170
And so on until you end up with a L, you know, as I guess GL of ZL.

00:08:01.170 --> 00:08:03.010
And then this is your prediction.

00:08:03.970 --> 00:08:07.650
And you notice that here you should use a vectorized implementation.

00:08:10.190 --> 00:08:17.150
It's just that this vectorized implementation processes 1000 examples at a time rather than

00:08:17.150 --> 00:08:18.830
5 million examples.

00:08:18.830 --> 00:08:28.990
Next, you compute the cost function, J, which I'm going to write as 1 over 1000,

00:08:28.990 --> 00:08:32.190
since here 1000 is the size of your little training set,

00:08:32.910 --> 00:08:41.150
sum from I equals 1 through L of really the, you know, loss of Y hat I, YI.

00:08:43.550 --> 00:08:51.710
And this notation for clarity refers to examples from the mini batch XT, YT.

00:08:53.630 --> 00:08:58.430
And then if you're using regularization, you can also have this regularization term,

00:08:59.390 --> 00:09:02.750
I guess maybe with a 2 in the denominator, times sum over L,

00:09:05.710 --> 00:09:08.110
Frobenius on the weight matrix squared.

00:09:08.110 --> 00:09:14.350
So because this is really the cost on just one mini batch, I'm going to index this cost

00:09:14.910 --> 00:09:18.350
J with a superscript T in curly braces.

00:09:18.910 --> 00:09:24.910
So you notice that everything we're doing is exactly the same as when we were previously

00:09:24.910 --> 00:09:30.350
implementing gradient descent, except that instead of doing it on X, Y, you're now doing it on

00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:39.070
X, T, Y, T. Next, you'd implement backprop to compute gradients

00:09:40.910 --> 00:09:49.230
with respect to really respect to this J, T. So you're still using only X, T, Y, T.

00:09:52.340 --> 00:10:01.380
And then you update the weights, you know, W, really every WL gets updated as WL minus alpha D,

00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:16.110
WL, and similarly for B. And so this is one pass through your training set

00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:19.150
using mini batch gradient descent.

00:10:19.150 --> 00:10:25.230
The code I've written down here is also called doing one epoch of training.

00:10:25.230 --> 00:10:31.870
And epoch is a word that just means a single pass through the training set.

00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:42.340
So whereas with batch gradient descent, a single pass through the training set allows you to

00:10:42.340 --> 00:10:47.540
take only one gradient descent step with mini batch gradient descent, a single pass through the

00:10:47.540 --> 00:10:52.980
training set that is one epoch allows you to take 5000 gradient descent steps.

00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:58.020
Now, of course, you want to take multiple passes through the training set, which you usually want

00:10:58.020 --> 00:11:02.820
to, you might want another for loop or another, you know, while loop out there.

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:08.180
So you keep taking passes through the training set until hopefully you converge or have approximately

00:11:08.180 --> 00:11:13.860
converged. When you have a large training set, mini batch gradient descent runs much faster than

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:18.580
batch gradient descent. And it's pretty much what everyone in deep learning will use when you're

00:11:18.580 --> 00:11:23.940
training on a large data set. In the next video, let's delve deeper into mini batch gradient descent,

00:11:23.940 --> 00:11:34.580
so you can get a better understanding of what it's doing and why it works so well.
